In Linux, pts stands for Pseudo-Terminal Slave. It represents a software-emulated terminal connection, unlike a standard physical or virtual console (tty). [1] (https://unix.stackexchange.com/questions/335992/what-is-the-difference-between-pts-and-tty-and-0), [2] (https://www.golinuxcloud.com/difference-between-pty-vs-tty-vs-pts-linux/)
Whenever you open a graphical terminal emulator (like GNOME Terminal, xterm, or Alacritty) or log into a system remotely (via SSH or tmux), the system dynamically allocates a pts device file, such as /dev/pts/0, /dev/pts/1, etc. [1] (https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man4/pts.4.html), [2] (https://biriukov.dev/docs/fd-pipe-session-terminal/4-terminals-and-pseudoterminals/), [3] (https://www.golinuxcloud.com/difference-between-pty-vs-tty-vs-pts-linux/)

How pts Works: The Master/Slave Pair

A pseudo-terminal (pty) always acts as a bidirectional pair consisting of a Master and a Slave: [1] (https://unix.stackexchange.com/questions/21280/difference-between-pts-and-tty), [2] (https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man4/pts.4.html)
1. The Multiplexer (/dev/ptmx): When an application (like an SSH server or terminal emulator) wants to create a new session, it opens the master multiplexer device /dev/ptmx. [1] (https://unix.stackexchange.com/questions/21280/difference-between-pts-and-tty), [2] (https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man4/pts.4.html)
2. The Slave (/dev/pts/X): The system automatically generates a corresponding slave file inside the /dev/pts/ directory. [1] (https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man4/pts.4.html), [2] (https://unix.stackexchange.com/questions/21280/difference-between-pts-and-tty)
3. Data Flow: The application (like sshd) interacts directly with the Master. Your interactive shell (like bash) interacts with the Slave. Data written to the slave is sent to the master as input, and vice versa. [1] (https://unix.stackexchange.com/questions/335992/what-is-the-difference-between-pts-and-tty-and-0), [2] (https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man4/pts.4.html)

Difference Between tty and pts


Feature	tty (Teletype / Teleprinter)	pts (Pseudo-Terminal Slave)
Nature	Physical or Core Virtual Terminal	Emulated Software Terminal
Typical Path	/dev/tty1, /dev/tty2, etc.	/dev/pts/0, /dev/pts/1, etc.
Triggered By	Direct native console access (e.g., Ctrl+Alt+F3)	SSH sessions, terminal tabs, tmux/screen


Useful Commands to Interact with pts

• Identify your current terminal: Running tty outputs the path of the specific pseudo-terminal assigned to your current shell: [1] (https://medium.com/@M4verick/how-i-went-from-confused-to-confident-understanding-ttys-ptys-ssh-and-tmux-d34252c0e452), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=moYrdwBovak)
bash
tty
# Output: /dev/pts/2
Use code with caution.
• See who is logged into which terminal: The who or w commands will list active users alongside their designated pts line: [1] (https://medium.com/@M4verick/how-i-went-from-confused-to-confident-understanding-ttys-ptys-ssh-and-tmux-d34252c0e452), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=moYrdwBovak)
bash
w
# Output shows USER, TTY (e.g., pts/0), FROM, LOGIN@, etc.
Use code with caution.
• View processes running under a specific terminal:bash
ps -t pts/2
Use code with caution.
• Send a text message directly to another terminal window: If you open two terminal windows (say pts/0 and pts/1), you can send text from one to the other by redirecting output: [1] (https://unix.stackexchange.com/questions/93531/what-is-stored-in-dev-pts-files-and-can-we-open-them)
bash
echo "Hello from the other side!" > /dev/pts/1
Use code with caution.

