1. Managing SSH PTY Allocation

When running non-interactive scripts over SSH, you often need to manually change how pseudo-terminals behave.
• Force PTY Allocation (-t): Use this when a remote command requires an interactive interface (like running sudo or top), but SSH refuses to create a PTY because it detects it is part of a script.bash
ssh -t user@remote_host "sudo apt update"
Use code with caution.
Note: Use -tt to force a PTY even if SSH has no local terminal attached.
• Disable PTY Allocation (-T): Use this for pure background scripts, automated file transfers, or port forwarding where an interactive prompt is unnecessary or could cause errors.bash
ssh -T user@remote_host < local_script.sh
Use code with caution.

2. Terminating Stuck pts User Sessions

If a remote user or a background process gets frozen or hangs up without properly disconnecting, you can force-close that specific pts session.
1. Identify the Target Session: Run w or who to find the exact pts number and the process ID (PID) of the shell running on it.bash
w
Use code with caution.
2. Find the Shell's Process ID: Look up the PID associated with that specific terminal.bash
ps -t pts/1 -o pid,comm
Use code with caution.
3. Kill the Session: Terminate the parent process (usually bash or sshd) running on that terminal. Start with a standard terminate signal (SIGTERM), and resort to a forced kill (SIGKILL) if it remains unresponsive.bash
# Polite kill (gives the shell a chance to close cleanly)
kill 15 <PID>

# Forced kill (instantly drops the connection)
kill -9 <PID>


