You will see and use these two terms in very different situations depending on how you are interacting with Linux.

When pts is Used

You deal with pts automatically 99% of the time during normal daily use. The system generates it whenever a software layer sits between you and the operating system.
• Opening Terminal Apps: Every time you open a terminal window inside a desktop environment (like GNOME Terminal, Konsole, or iTerm).
• Remote Connections: When you log into a remote server via SSH.
• Terminal Multiplexers: When using tools like tmux or screen, each new split or pane gets its own pts device.
• When to type it: You use the pts path (e.g., /dev/pts/3) when you want to target a specific user session to send them a message or kill a stuck shell.

When You Have to Use tty

You use tty when dealing with the core, raw operating system interface, bypassing the graphical interface entirely.
• System Recovery: If your desktop environment crashes or freezes completely, you can press Ctrl + Alt + F3 (through F6) to drop into a raw text-only screen. This is a direct physical console, managed by /dev/tty3.
• Embedded Hardware: If you connect directly to a server or a microcomputer (like a Raspberry Pi) using a physical serial cable or a monitor plugged straight into the motherboard.
• Scripting Security: In shell scripts, the term tty is used as a command or flag to check if a human is actively typing into the script ([ -t 0 ]), or if the script is just running blindly in the background.

